 "title": "The Art of Communication",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator explains a communication strategy of speaking broadly about things you like and narrowly about things you dislike. The main lesson is that this approach helps you connect with more people while minimizing offense.",
 "key_points": [
  "Speak very broadly about things you like.",
  "Speak very narrowly about things you don't like.",
  "Example of speaking narrowly: saying 'I don't care for the texture of raw red onions when they're sliced excessively thick in a salad' offends far fewer people than saying 'I don't like Mediterranean food'.",
  "Example of speaking broadly: saying 'I love video games' instead of immediately saying 'I love Black Ops' lets you find common ground with someone who might play 2K."
 ],
 "content": "The Art of Communication. Speak very broadly about the things that you like and very narrowly about the things that you don't like. Welcome to thoughts on the pot. Here's an example. If you say I don't like Mediterranean food, two things. One is that if the other person even remotely likes Mediterranean food, they're going to judge you for it. And the other is that there's probably something about Mediterranean food that you don't like which you should try and articulate. If you were to speak narrowly and say um I don't care for the texture of raw red onions when they're sliced excessively thick in a salad, the number of people that you offend reduces drastically. On the other hand, when you're talking about something that you like, you want to cast the net as widely as you can. If you were to say oh I love Black Ops off the rip, you may not connect with someone as much as you would if you said, oh I love gaming. And then they go, oh me too. And then you ask him what he games. And then possibly he plays 2K, which you also play. All of a sudden, y'all are making plans for Saturday. Text shown on screen: The Art of Communication, Very, Speak, Broadly, about the things, and, very, narrowly, about the things, that you don't like, welcome, to, thoughts on the pot, Here's an example, if, you, say, Mediterranean food, 2 things, One is that if the, other person even, remotely likes, Mediterranean food, they're gonna, judge you for it, And the other is that, there's, probably, something about, Mediterranean food, that you don't like, you should try and articulate, If you were to speak, narrowly, and, say, and, umm, I don't care for the, texture of, raw red onions, when they're sliced, excessively, thick in a, salad, The number of people, that you offend, reduces, drastically, When you're talking, about something that, you like, want to cast the net, as widely as you can, If you were to say, oh, I, love Black Ops, off the rip, you may not connect with, someone as much as, you would if you said, oh, I, love video games, love gaming, And then they go, me, too, And then you ask, him what he games, And then possibly he, plays 2K, which you, also play, Did we just become best friends?, All of a sudden, y'all, for Saturday.",
